 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Supermarket hack attack 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Hackers are attacking supermarket fridge and freezer systems across Europe, raising concerns of similar episodes in the U.S., Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 👾 Dozens of European supermarkets were hit with suspected cyberattacks overnight, spoiling food and threatening costly losses.

 Grocery chains Ahold Delhaize and Colruyt said incidents affected stores in Belgium and Luxembourg, Reuters reports. Swedish chains ICA and Coop reported similar disruptions. 🇫🇷 The incidents follow similar attacks in France last month, where hackers seized control of hundreds of refrigeration units.

 It's unclear whether the attacks are connected. Workers empty spoiled frozen goods today at a supermarket in Sjöbo, Sweden. Photo: Johan Nilsson/TT News Agency via Getty Images How it works: Many grocery stores use internet-connected devices, letting maintenance workers remotely monitor temperatures.

 Hackers can exploit software flaws or stolen login credentials to change temperatures or activate defrost modes via those devices. 🥶 Many U.S. supermarkets use fridge and freezer units like those affected in Europe, raising concerns that they could be vulnerable to similar attacks.

 The Pentagon is also investigating whether cyberattacks caused freezer malfunctions in at least six U.S. military commissaries in August. What to know . 

 
 
